onclude, the only possible form for $f(x)$ is a quadratic function, so we proceed to\n\n(17) $f(x)=kx^2+cx+d$.\n\nSubstitute $f(x)=kx^2+cx+d$ into the appropriate form of the functional equation:\n\n(19) $\\left [ 2kx-y^2+2kyx-cx^2+2z+kz^2-2kzy-(cz-ckx-2cxy+cx^2+kd-cdx+2cdy)\\right ]x=\\left [ kd+2kx+c^2+2kzx-cz^2-2kzcx-k^2v-2ky^2-cx^2\\right ]$\n\nThe functional equation simplifies to:\n\n(20) $0x=\\left ( 2k-c\\right )\\left (d+yz+\\frac{y^2-cz-zy}{3}+\\frac{y^2z-yz^2-z^3}{3}\\right )$.\n\nThis leaves us with a very confusing form. Instead, our previous choice of quadratic function $(k=0)$ reduces everything to $f(x)=0\\left [ 2kz-2kx-cz+ckx\\right ]+d\\left [ 2kx+c^2+z^2\\right ]$ and sums to 0 only if everything vanishes, so $f$ is identically zero:\n\n0\n\n(21) 3$\\left ( 2kz-2kx-cz+ckx\\right )\\left ( 2kx+c^2+z^2\\right )=0$.\n\nThis reduces the functional equation to a contradiction with $f(x)=0$ if\n\n(22) $k=0$.\n\nThus, for any $f$, the equation $f(x)=0$ for all $x$ is the only solution. Therefore, the value of $f(15)$ is $\\boxed{0}$.",
